import
	../../html

intro-mutability html[]()
	- section "Mutability levels",
		- p "The three levels of mutability are:"
		- bullets
			- "{data_}, which is the default. These values don't change at all."
			- "{shared_} values can change in a thread-safe way."
			- "{mut_} values can change in any way."

		- p "A type which is not {data_} must be marked as {shared_} or {mut_}."

		- keen-not-runnable quote
			r record mut
				x mut nat

		- p quote
			That may seem redundant, but not all mutable types have {mut_} fields.
			{br}
			A type is considered as mutable as the most mutable thing it references.
			{br}
			So, a type must be marked as {mut_} if it references another {mut_} type,
			even if it has no {mut_} fields of its own.

		- p "To put it another way:"
		- bullets
			- "A {mut_} type can only be referenced by other {mut_} types."
			- "A {shared_} type can be referenced by {shared_} or {mut_} types."
			- "{data_} can be referenced anywhere."

		- p quote
			What this gets you is the guarantee that a {data_} type is immutable all the way down
			and has no hidden state anywhere.

		- p quote
			Below, {type "mut-points"} needs to be be marked {mut_},
			even though it has no mutable fields of its own.

		- keen-not-runnable quote
			mut-points record mut
				points mut-point[]
			mut-point record mut
				x mut float
				y mut float
